B.a1P={"Why does an LED have one longer leg?":0,[u.P]:1,"Why does an LED blow without a resistor?":2,"How does code make an LED blink?":3}
B.Zz=new A.y(B.a1P,["The longer leg is the Anode (+)! Manufacturers make it longer so you can easily tell the positive lead apart from the shorter Cathode (\u2212) lead.","Nothing harmful happens at 5V, but it will not light up! An LED is a diode: it completely blocks reverse current.","An LED has almost zero internal resistance once turned on. Connecting it directly across 5V allows 85+ mA to rush in, overheating and melting the microscopic silicon wire.","By switching an Arduino pin between HIGH (5V) and LOW (0V) with a delay(500) pause inside void loop()!"],t.w)
B.YO=new A.y(B.ae,["LED (Light Emitting Diode)","Why does an LED only glow in one direction and need a resistor?","A Light Emitting Diode (LED) is a semiconductor that converts electric current into photons of light. It works strictly in forward bias: current enters the longer positive anode leg (+) and exits the shorter cathode leg (\u2212).","Real-Life Memory Analogy: Like a one-way illuminated turnstile at a theme park! People (electrons) can only walk through in one direction, and every time someone crosses, a victory glow lights up above!","Cause & Effect: Why does an LED need a resistor? Without a 330 \u03a9 resistor, 85 mA surges through, blowing the tiny semiconductor junction in a puff of smoke. With 330 \u03a9, current is limited to a safe 9.1 mA!","Real-World Everyday Places: Traffic signals, smartphone flashlights, car brake lights, TV remotes (infrared LED), and robot indicator lights.","When to Use It: Whenever your robot needs a visual status indicator (Power ON, Sensor Triggered, Obstacle Warning, or Blinking Alert).",B.T3,B.Vi,B.Zz],t.R)
